ction 2, and every source is in section 8, except item 3 above. I read the Claude Code team's article through a page summary and did not check every quotation word for word. Its review-round figures (65% to 78%, 8.9% to 1.5%, 2.08 to 1.32), the doubling of merged changes, the fall in reverts of more than 40 percent, the three-round step back and the fifty-round case all match the note.\n- Description, not ranking: section 4 states what dap records and what it does not. The sentence at lines 190-192, \"no counterpart in the three products\", is a factual comparison. It is not a ranking, and it suits a research note that names products only as sources.\n- Section 6 states the decisions as Hugh's of 26 October 2026, and the status line says the note commissions nothing. Section 7 is a proposed order.\n- Prose lines are at most 79 characters. The longer lines are table rows (202-211, 280-282) and two URLs that cannot be broken (309, 311).\n- At the head, check-notes a97c7425..c81b168d reports 0 warnings, and check-doc-links reports 705 relative links checked and 0 broken (run on an exported tree of the head).\n\nArchitecture: sound as analysis. The note places each new practice correctly: as lane data (the finding item, the round cap, a tier-0 extent) or as a platform version (who may land, who counts as a reviewer). It keeps the record's existing rules: approval per exact manifest, no agent approval, controller tracing. A revert recorded as a link kind is lane data, because the change lane already has a link item (change.ts:183). Note for the planner: kernel section 7.1 (lines 328-329) lets a new version of an existing platform definition proceed only if its head says it adds one to K1's count. The destination and membership versions in section 7 owe that line when they are filed.\n\nSecurity: no code, configuration or authority change. The decisions keep \"an agent's approval never counts as a person's\" and the controller rule from the roles note. Decision 1 replaces